{"mode":"probe_at_tier","concept":"information-hiding","target_tier":"Apply","content":"Sure — let's test that idea with one scenario, and I'll leave the call to you.

Suppose `internalRecords` is declared `private`, and the module offers `getRecords()` that returns it directly. Now imagine the module later swaps its storage from an `ArrayList` to a `HashMap` (or to a database) to make lookups faster.

Under that planned change, what would a caller that already holds the value returned by `getRecords()` be able to do to the module's own data — and what does that tell you about whether `private` alone did the hiding here?","format":null,"rubric":null,"concept_tags":null,"sources":["SEBook/tools/java.md — Object-Oriented Programming > Information Hiding > The Getter/Setter Fallacy (https://github.com/tobiasduerschmid/tobiasduerschmid.github.io/blob/24db6bf0d4e4ebfa1331e6c772e1844cbaf2d9aa/SEBook/tools/java.md)","SEBook/designprinciples/informationhiding.md — The Mechanics > Information Hiding ≠ Encapsulation ≠ "Make It Private" (https://github.com/tobiasduerschmid/tobiasduerschmid.github.io/blob/24db6bf0d4e4ebfa1331e6c772e1844cbaf2d9aa/SEBook/designprinciples/informationhiding.md)","SEBook/designprinciples/informationhiding.md — Applying and Evaluating > Classify the Leak Before You Fix It (https://github.com/tobiasduerschmid/tobiasduerschmid.github.io/blob/24db6bf0d4e4ebfa1331e6c772e1844cbaf2d9aa/SEBook/designprinciples/informationhiding.md)"],"coverage_gap":null}

